{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויצא בן אשה ישראלית", :translit "vayetze ben ishah yisra'elit", :gloss "and went out the son of an Israelite woman"} {:he "המקלל", :translit "hamekalel", :gloss "the blasphemer"} {:he "ביום השבת", :translit "beyom ha-shabbat", :gloss "on the day of the Sabbath"} {:he "מקושש", :translit "mekoshesh", :gloss "gatherer"} {:he "שהמקלל ומקושש היו בזמן א'", :translit "she-hamekalel u-mekoshesh hayu bezeman echad", :gloss "that the blasphemer and the gatherer were at one time"} {:he "המחלל שבת ככופר בעיקר", :translit "ha-mechalel shabbat ke-kofeir be-ikar", :gloss "the one who desecrates Sabbath is like one who denies the fundamental principle"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut: juxtaposition of the blasphemer pericope to the Sabbath-gatherer pericope yields two inferences"}], :frame {:text "The gathering of wood by the mekoshesh on the Sabbath is narrated in Numbers 15:32–36. The Torah juxtaposes the blasphemer episode immediately after Sabbath laws in Leviticus 24:8–9, suggesting a link. Shabbat desecration is equated with denial of fundamental principle: as Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael on Exodus 31:14 states that one who profanes Sabbath is considered to have denied the Exodus and the creation. The view that the blasphemer and the gatherer occurred at the same time is found in Leviticus Rabbah 32:1.", :sources ["Numbers 15:32–36" "Leviticus 24:8–9" "Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael on Exodus 31:14" "Leviticus Rabbah 32:1"]}} {:quotes [{:he "וינצו", :translit "vayinatzu", :gloss "and they fought"} {:he "וינצו במחנה", :translit "vayinatzu ba-machaneh", :gloss "and they fought in the camp"} {:he "וינצו שניהם בשדה", :translit "vayinatzu shneihem ba-sadeh", :gloss "and they fought both of them in the field"} {:he "שיצא חייב מבית דינו של משה", :translit "she-yatza chayav mi-beit dino shel mosheh", :gloss "that he went out liable from the court of Moshe"} {:he "שרצה ליטע אהלו במחנה דן", :translit "she-ratzah litei'a oholo be-machaneh dan", :gloss "that he wanted to pitch his tent in the camp of Dan"} {:he "וינצו בשדה", :translit "vayinatzu ba-sadeh", :gloss "and they fought in the field"} {:he "על עסקי שדה", :translit "al askei sadeh", :gloss "on account of a matter of field"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וינצו במחנה" "וינצו שניהם בשדה"]}], :frame {:text "The view that the blasphemer's mother was from the tribe of Dan and that the fight arose because he sought to pitch his tent in the camp of Dan, only to be judged liable by Moshe's court, is found in Vayikra Rabbah 32:5. Since his mother Shelomit was of the tribe of Dan (Leviticus 24:11) while his father was an Egyptian (Exodus 2:11ff.), his tribal affiliation was disputed, leading to the quarrel. The doubling of the word 'וינצו' signals that there were two stages of conflict — first in the camp, then in the field.", :sources ["Leviticus 24:11" "Exodus 2:11" "Leviticus Rabbah 32:5"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut: juxtaposition of the blasphemer pericope to the Sabbath-gatherer pericope yields two inferences", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וינצו במחנה" "וינצו שניהם בשדה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "וינצו במחנה", :refs ["Leviticus 24:10"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "וינצו שניהם בשדה", :refs ["2 Samuel 14:6"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:25:28.337575231Z", :model "glm-5.2"}